          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m with m=2-4 reduces FF count vs full pipelining while still achieving 1 result/cycle throughput. The burst of 32 requests must complete in ~32 cycles, so 1/cycle throughput is mandatory. m=2-4 keeps latency manageable (ceil(N/m)+2 cycles) while cutting register area. Same accuracy parameter sweep as pipelined."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires 32 results within 0.44us of a 1us tick, which at a realistic ~74MHz Fmax means ~32 cycles of processing time. Only pipelined and pipelined_m families achieve 1 result/cycle throughput, making them the only viable families for this bursty workload. The throughput constraint (>=32 Msps) further confirms 1/cycle is needed. For accuracy, max_abs_err <= 2^-12 (~0.000244) is the binding constraint; CORDIC with N=12-16 iterations and W=14-18 bits should meet this. I focus data_width 14-18 and n_iter 12-20 to bracket the accuracy/area trade-off. frac_guard 0-4 and angle_guard -2..2 sweep the remaining accuracy knobs. For pipelined_m, m=2-4 balances FF reduction against latency (must stay well under 32 cycles to fit the burst window). The budget is split 55/45 between the two families since pipelined is the baseline and pipelined_m offers the main area-reduction opportunity."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The exploration has successfully identified a feasible Pareto front that satisfies all system and accuracy constraints. The best design (pipelined_m, data_width=18, n_iter=16, m=4) achieves 13.02 bits of accuracy (well above the 12-bit requirement) with a resource cost of 1236 LUTs+FFs, which is significantly below the hypervolume reference point of 4000. The throughput (97.8 MSPS) and system latency (0.165 us p99 batch) comfortably meet the 32 MSPS and 0.44 us constraints. Since this is the final round, the current front is sufficient for selection."
}
